-- Plan 61 leaf 4: a cycle among `where` bindings, which has no order to emit.
--
-- The one place Redox is deliberately **stricter than Haskell**, and §6 says
-- so because a reader porting Haskell will meet it: `a = b + 1; b = a + 1` is
-- accepted there and diverges. Refusing it at compile time is a better
-- outcome and it is still a divergence.
--
-- A binding in a cycle is parameterless **by construction** since leaf 0 —
-- anything taking a parameter is a function entry, which emits as a nested
-- `fn` and is order-independent in Rust — so this needs no arity test, which
-- is what made leaf 3 free. The help says what to do about it: give one of
-- them a parameter and the cycle becomes recursion.

f : u64 -> u64
f n = a
  where
    a = b + n
    b = a + 1
